Content within two device pixels of a scroll container's bounds doesn't scroll.

Taffy rounds each node's location on its own but its size against its
absolute position, and gpui sums the rounded locations, so a child's far
edge can land a device pixel past its parent's even though the unrounded
layout fits. The unrounded padding added to the content size adds up to
another. The scroll max picked that up as real overflow, and a container
sized to its content got a full-length scrollbar and a one-pixel wheel
scroll. Whether it hits depends on the fractional offset of the container
in the tree, so one panel's context menu can show it while another's with
the same rows doesn't. A sweep of offsets, row heights and paddings at
scale 2 measured 1 device pixel with integral padding and 1.6 with
fractional padding.

Diffed against the tree with every earlier patch applied.

--- a/src/elements/div.rs	2006-07-23 19:21:28.000000000 -0600
+++ b/src/elements/div.rs	2026-10-01 17:15:13.558754807 -0600
@@ -1744,8 +1744,20 @@
             // high for the maximum scroll, we round the scroll max to 2 decimal
             // places here.
             let padded_content_size = self.content_size + padding_size;
+
+            // rox patch: Taffy rounds a node's location on its own but its size
+            // against its absolute position, and the padding above is unrounded,
+            // so content that fits can still land up to two device pixels past
+            // the bounds. That much overflow is rounding, not content.
+            let slack = px(2.) / window.scale_factor();
             let scroll_max = (padded_content_size - bounds.size)
-                .map(round_to_two_decimals)
+                .map(|overflow| {
+                    if overflow < slack {
+                        px(0.)
+                    } else {
+                        round_to_two_decimals(overflow)
+                    }
+                })
                 .max(&Default::default());
             // Clamp scroll offset in case scroll max is smaller now (e.g., if children
             // were removed or the bounds became larger).
